<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth was a federated state created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. It was one of the largest states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, notable for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elected monarchy</a>, powerful noble parliament and relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>. Weakened by internal paralysis and foreign intervention, it was partitioned out of existence by its neighbours by 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth.jpg" alt="Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olek Remesz (wiki-pl: Orem, commons: Orem) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chor%C4%85giew_kr%C3%B3lewska_kr%C3%B3la_Zygmunta_III_Wazy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cossack Hetmanate, officially the Zaporozhian Host, was a stratocratic state built by Registered Cossacks in Dnieper Ukraine out of the Khmelnytsky Uprising of 1648 against the <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Raids into <b>Poland-Lithuania</b> and Muscovy seized men, women and children who were sold through the slave market of Caffa, under direct Ottoman rule, to buyers across the Ottoman Empire and the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its dynastic union with Poland, begun at Krewo in 1385, produced the Jagiellonian dynasty, which at times ruled Poland, Lithuania, Bohemia and Hungary, and ultimately the <b>Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ivan IV’s capture of Kazan in 1552 was followed by the long Livonian War of 1558–1583, which began with conquests in Livonia but ended in defeat by <b>Poland-Lithuania</b> under Stephen Báthory and by Sweden, with the loss of Russia’s gains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1652 a single deputy in the Sejm of the <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b> could end a session and void all it had passed by declaring “I do not allow”; this noble privilege, the liberum veto, was abolished only in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</b> adopted the Constitution of 3 May 1791, often called the first modern written constitution in Europe, and revolutionary France adopted one of its own in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
